// v0.3-M3b compile-error gallery
//
// M3b adds cross-module suspension codegen. Calling any imported suspending
// function is universally rejected at compile time until M3e ships full
// FrameLayout serialization across the export table.
//
// WHY the universal reject: the typeck-side frame analysis that tried to predict
// which cross-module cases were safe proved wrong five times — number crossing-local,
// transitive×caller-frame, and three other combos all slipped through and crashed
// silently. A guard that rejects everything cannot miss a case.
//
// ALL cross-module suspending calls are CROSS-MODULE errors — they require a two- or
// three-file project to trigger. Each class is exercised by a fixture project under
// `crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_loud_reject_*/` and covered by
// integration tests. This gallery file documents the error classes.
//
// Single-file errors added in M3b (none — all guards are cross-module):
// There are no new single-file error classes in M3b.
//
// To see the universal-reject diagnostic, run any of these fixture projects:
//   ynz run crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_cross_module_suspending_caller
//   ynz run crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_cross_module_int_return
//   ynz run crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_cross_module_errors_capable
//   ynz run crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_transitive_suspend
//   ynz run crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_crossing_local_cross_module
//   ynz run crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_loud_reject_reexport
//   ynz run crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_loud_reject_shape_crossing
//   ynz run crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_loud_reject_ec_transitive

// -- M3b Guard: CrossModuleImportedSuspendingCall (universal) --
//
// WHY: CrossModuleImportedSuspendingCall — calling any imported function that
// suspends (directly or transitively) is rejected at compile time. The caller's
// state machine needs the callee's full saved-state layout — exact slot positions
// for every variable that survives a pause, plus any nested calls — to embed the
// callee's saved-state block at the right offset. Carrying only a scalar size
// across the module boundary cannot reconstruct that layout.
//
// The diagnostic emitted (WHAT / WHAT-INSTEAD / WHY):
//
//   WHAT: Calling `<name>` across a module boundary isn't supported yet —
//         it (or a function it calls) suspends, and composing the caller's
//         saved state needs the callee's exact memory layout, which isn't
//         carried across modules yet.
//
//   WHAT INSTEAD: Move `<name>` into this module, or avoid suspending it
//                 across the boundary, until v0.3-M3e ships cross-module
//                 frame-layout serialization.
//
//   WHY: The caller's state machine needs the callee's full slot map — exact
//        positions for every variable that survives a pause, plus any nested
//        calls — to embed the callee's saved-state block at the right offset.
//        A scalar size alone cannot reconstruct that. Calling with a wrong
//        layout silently corrupts memory; a clean error here is the safe floor.
//        See design/future/cross-module-frame-serialization.md for the M3e plan.
//
// The guard fires for ALL cross-module suspending call shapes:
//
//   (a) Direct export: module A exports a function that calls sleep() directly.
//       Fixture: crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_cross_module_suspending_caller/
//
//   (b) Int-return export: same as (a) but the suspending function returns int.
//       Fixture: crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_cross_module_int_return/
//
//   (c) Errors-capable export: the suspending function is declared `-> T errors`.
//       Fixture: crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_cross_module_errors_capable/
//
//   (d) Transitive suspend: the exported function calls an inner non-exported
//       function that suspends. The outer function is marked suspends=true
//       transitively and is still rejected.
//       Fixture: crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_transitive_suspend/
//
//   (e) Crossing local in caller: the caller has a variable that crosses a wait
//       boundary in addition to calling the imported suspending function.
//       Fixture: crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_crossing_local_cross_module/
//
//   (f) Re-export / multi-level transitive (3-module chain):
//       Module B imports a suspending function from A and re-exports its own
//       function that calls it. Module C imports from B.
//       Fixture: crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_loud_reject_reexport/
//
//   (g) Shape-typed crossing local in the callee:
//       The exported suspending function has a local of a shape type that
//       crosses the suspension boundary.
//       Fixture: crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_loud_reject_shape_crossing/
//
//   (h) Errors-capable export that suspends transitively:
//       An exported `-> T errors` function calls an inner function that suspends.
//       The EC staging slot interacts with sub-frame offsets.
//       Fixture: crates/ynz-driver/tests/fixtures/v0_3_m3b_loud_reject_ec_transitive/
//
// All eight emit a WHAT/WHAT-INSTEAD/WHY diagnostic (exit 1) instead of silently
// crashing with SIGILL (exit 132). M3e ships full FrameLayout serialization and
// removes the universal reject (design/future/cross-module-frame-serialization.md).

// -- M3b Phase-4 Error Class: mutate-through-share-param (single-file) --
//
// WHY: ShareParamMutation — auto-parallelization treats a `share` argument as a
// read-only borrow that cannot conflict with a concurrent write. That soundness
// premise (design/concurrency.md line 651) only holds if the compiler ENFORCES
// that a `share` parameter's body cannot change the value. A `share`-param body
// that mutates a field (or an element) is now a compile error.
//
// A bare parameter (no modifier) that mutates IS legal — the compiler figures out
// the effective `lend`. Only an explicit `share` is the contradiction.
//
// Diagnostic (WHAT / WHAT-INSTEAD / WHY):
//   WHAT: `pickle` is declared `share` — read-only — so its fields cannot be changed.
//   WHAT INSTEAD: Change the parameter to `lend pickle: Order` if this function
//                 needs to modify it.
//   WHY: A `share` parameter is a read-only borrow: the caller keeps ownership and
//        trusts the value is unchanged after the call. When a function modifies a
//        value, declare `lend` so the change is visible at every call site.

shape Order {
  quantity: int
}

function bumpQuantity(share pickle: Order) -> nothing {
  // WHY: ShareParamMutation — `pickle` is declared `share` (read-only); writing
  // a field of it contradicts the read-only borrow. Declare `lend pickle: Order`
  // to mutate. Bare `pickle: Order` would also compile (the compiler figures out lend).
  pickle.quantity = pickle.quantity + 1
}

// -- M3b Phase-4 Error Class: mutate-through-share-param (TRANSITIVE) --
//
// WHY: ShareParamMutation (transitive) — the read-only guarantee holds even when the
// mutation happens through a callee. `relayOrder` declares `ticket` as `share` but passes
// it to `stamp`, a bare-parameter function that mutates its argument (effective `lend`).
// `relayOrder` is therefore lending `ticket` onward — a compile error. The transitive
// effective-ownership fixpoint catches this; the direct-mutation check above does not,
// because `stamp`'s declared modifier is bare, not `lend`.
//
// Diagnostic (WHAT / WHAT-INSTEAD / WHY):
//   WHAT: `ticket` is declared `share` (read-only) but it is modified through `stamp`.
//   WHAT INSTEAD: Change the parameter to `lend ticket: Order` — the function (directly
//                 or through a call) writes to it.
//   WHY: A `share` parameter is a read-only borrow; the caller trusts the value is
//        unchanged. Because `stamp` modifies `ticket`, this function lends it onward —
//        declare `lend` so the write is visible at every call site.

function stamp(order: Order) -> nothing {
  order.quantity = order.quantity + 10
}

function relayOrder(share ticket: Order) -> nothing {
  // WHY: ShareParamMutation (transitive) — `ticket` is `share` (read-only) but `stamp`
  // mutates it. Declare `lend ticket: Order` to lend it onward for mutation.
  stamp(ticket)
}

// -- M3b Phase-4 Error Class: mutate-through-share-param (COLLECTION METHOD) --
//
// WHY: ShareParamMutation (collection) — an in-place collection mutator (`array.add`,
// `array.set`, `map.set`/`remove`/`clear`, `fixed.set`) writes its receiver in place, the
// same as a direct element assign. A `share` parameter is a read-only borrow, so calling a
// mutating method on it contradicts that promise and is a compile error. A read method
// (`.get`, `.count`, `.contains`) leaves the receiver unchanged and is always allowed.
//
// Diagnostic (WHAT / WHAT-INSTEAD / WHY):
//   WHAT: `lineItems` is declared `share` — read-only — so its elements cannot be changed.
//   WHAT INSTEAD: Change the parameter to `lend lineItems: array<int>` if this function
//                 needs to modify it.
//   WHY: A `share` parameter is a read-only borrow: the caller keeps ownership and trusts
//        the value is unchanged after the call. When a function modifies a value, declare
//        `lend` so the change is visible at every call site.

function appendLineItem(share lineItems: array<int>) -> nothing {
  // WHY: ShareParamMutation (collection) — `.add()` appends in place, mutating the receiver.
  // `lineItems` is `share` (read-only); declare `lend lineItems: array<int>` to grow it.
  lineItems.add(42)
}
